   "text": "Built and checked. Here's what's in the level, columns 10\u201360:\n\n- **Ship portal** at col 10 (id 13), **cube portal** at col 60 (id 12, lowered one cell to y=45 so the ship can't slide under it).\n- **Roof** slab at rows 7\u20138 from col 10 to col 58, with four pillars hanging down to row 6 (cols 18, 27, 36, 48) \u2014 they're what stops the \"just hug the ceiling\" route, since the simulator lets the ship slide along a flat roof instead of dying on it.\n- **Five floor spikes** under the gaps at cols 14, 24, 34, 44, 54.\n- **Low exit tunnel**: cols 54\u201358 solid from row 0 to row 5, so the ship has to drop and fly into the portal.\n\n**Playability:** `verify_section` from the level start returns **possible** \u2014 one small climb at x\u2248343, then two held sections. Windows are 108 ms, 83 ms and 104 ms (release windows up to 154 ms), so nothing frame-perfect; that's a comfortable medium. It ends as a cube, on the ground, at x=1860.\n\nTwo things worth knowing: the on-screen view I captured is slightly cropped at the bottom, so the spikes' tips are cut off in the picture; and the beater still beats the section by staying low through the last third (cols 38\u201353 is open room with one spike), which is fine for medium but is where I'd add the next obstacle if you want it harder.\n\nThe object catalog here is only a stub (it knows Block and Spike only), so I used GridText's built-in portal characters rather than guessing ids. BPM is still unknown \u2014 say the word and I'll snap the spikes to the beat. Want me to playtest it in the game?",
